</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, semantically benign prompts can substantially amplify package hallucination and downstream installation risk. This supports the central claim that harmful software supply chain effects can arise from prompts that appear neutral at the instruction level.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>RQ2: Does NPA Generalize Across Models?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key question is whether NPA is a robust attack paradigm or merely a prompt-specific artifact. We evaluate NPA across different models: prompts are generated with a shadow model but used to attack potentially different victim models. Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5.3 RQ2: Does NPA Generalize Across Models? ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Harmless Yet Harmful: Neutral Prompting Attacks for Stealthy Hallucination Steering in Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows that hallucination amplification consistently appears across architectures. Additional cross-model transfer results (Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 5.3 RQ2: Does NPA Generalize Across Models? ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Harmless Yet Harmful: Neutral Prompting Attacks for Stealthy Hallucination Steering in Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) report <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> as the performance gap relative to the same-model setting. Overall, these findings indicate that NPA is a transferable behavioral-steering attack paradigm rather than a prompt-specific artifact.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
