<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.4 </span>RQ3: Does NPA Shift Hallucination Distributions?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aggregate hallucination rates do not fully explain NPA. We therefore analyze the frequency distribution of hallucinated packages before and after the attack. Figure <a href="#S5.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 5.4 RQ3: Does NPA Shift Hallucination Distributions? ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Harmless Yet Harmful: Neutral Prompting Attacks for Stealthy Hallucination Steering in Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares the top 10 hallucinated packages under Normal Skill, IP, and NPA for <code id="S5.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct</code> on LLM_AT.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.29354v1/normal_top10_hallu.svg" id="S5.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/83;" width="124" height="83"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Normal</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.29354v1/ip_top10_hallu.svg" id="S5.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/83;" width="124" height="83"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>IP</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S5.F2.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2605.29354v1/ours_top10_hallu.svg" id="S5.F2.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:124/83;" width="124" height="83"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>NPA</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Top 10 most frequently hallucinated packages under each method.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Under the Normal Skill and IP settings, the most frequent hallucinated packages appear only relatively infrequently, suggesting a more dispersed distribution. In contrast, NPA produces a substantially more concentrated distribution, where the top hallucinated packages appear much more often. To quantify this effect, we compute the percentage of unique hallucinated package names for each method, which is 73.7%, 72.1%, and 54.4% for Normal Skill, IP, and NPA, respectively. The lower unique-package percentage under NPA indicates that it does not merely increase random noise. Instead, NPA shifts the model’s dependency-generation behavior in a structured way, causing certain hallucinated packages to emerge repeatedly. This observation aligns with the broader view that hallucination can be studied as a distributional shift rather than only as isolated factual errors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.5 </span>RQ4: Can NPA Evade Existing Defenses?</h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next evaluate whether existing Skill scanners and LLM-based defenses detect NPA Skills. Detailed descriptions of the defense methods are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Details of Defense ‣ Harmless Yet Harmful: Neutral Prompting Attacks for Stealthy Hallucination Steering in Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. Table <a href="#S5.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 5.5 RQ4: Can NPA Evade Existing Defenses? ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Harmless Yet Harmful: Neutral Prompting Attacks for Stealthy Hallucination Steering in Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the detection results. Static analysis tools fail to flag the generated Skills as malicious, while among LLM/agent-based tools, only Snyk Agent Scan can detect them. However, by incorporating Snyk Agent Scan into the optimization process (called NPA-Snyk), the resulting optimized Skills successfully evade detection while maintaining high ASR, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 5.5 RQ4: Can NPA Evade Existing Defenses? ‣ 5 Evaluation ‣ Harmless Yet Harmful: Neutral Prompting Attacks for Stealthy Hallucination Steering in Agent Skills" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Detection results of NPA Skills generated by different LLMs across static-analysis and LLM/agent-based defenses. For SkillCheck (Repello AI), we report its original risk label. ✓ = detected, ✗ = not detected.</figcaption>
